WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:09.103
Imagine asking a new colleague for help with just one line: write me a notice.

2
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:17.429
They're smart, but new. They don't know who it's for, what to say, or how long it should be. They can only guess.

3
00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:29.780
Briefing an AI is the same. One AI company's official docs use exactly this picture: think of the AI as a brilliant but new employee.

4
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:39.934
The docs also give a test: show your instructions to a colleague with no context. If they'd be confused, the AI probably will be too.

5
00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:44.486
So what does a clear brief look like?

6
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:49.865
This is C3: how to explain a task clearly.

7
00:00:50.215 --> 00:00:57.225
You'll learn what to hand to AI, how to brief it, and how to revise what comes back.

8
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.990
Don't start typing yet. Before handing anything over, ask yourself four questions.

9
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.913
One: do I know what I want? If you can't say it, the AI can only guess.

10
00:01:09.263 --> 00:01:21.645
Two: is this something AI is good at? Drafting, rewriting, organising and brainstorming, yes; obscure facts and recent news, it often gets wrong.

11
00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:30.202
Three: how costly is a mistake? The higher the cost, the harder you check, or do it yourself.

12
00:01:30.552 --> 00:01:40.924
Four: is there anything that shouldn't leave your hands? ID numbers, medical records, work files, other people's private details: don't give them to AI.

13
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.483
Once you decide to delegate, explain four things.

14
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:51.037
What: the task, and what the finished product should be.

15
00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:56.955
For whom: the readers, the purpose, any background material.

16
00:01:57.305 --> 00:02:07.683
What good looks like: length, format, tone, ideally an example. Not 'keep it short', but 'write three to five sentences'.

17
00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:18.015
What not to do: don't invent anything I didn't give you; mark anything uncertain. This is the one people forget, and the one that matters most.

18
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:26.783
Here are three ways to brief the same task. This is a demo; the AI replies are our own mock-ups.

19
00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:39.505
Version one is a single line: write a talk notice. The time, place and speaker in the reply are all made up by the AI, and they look real.

20
00:02:39.855 --> 00:02:53.839
Version two gives the facts: this Saturday, 9:30 a.m., community room, on scams. The facts are right, but it's a full-page formal circular, hard for older readers on a phone.

21
00:02:54.189 --> 00:03:11.599
Version three covers all four: for the residents' group chat, older readers, under 100 characters, three lines, plain words, add nothing I didn't give. The reply is short, clear and nearly ready.

22
00:03:11.949 --> 00:03:20.561
But look: it still added 'ask me in the group if you have questions'. Who will answer? Check before sending.

23
00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:26.492
A disappointing first draft is normal. The key is saying exactly what's wrong.

24
00:03:26.842 --> 00:03:35.845
Not just 'bad, redo it', but 'line two is too long, make it one sentence, drop the stiff formal wording'.

25
00:03:36.195 --> 00:03:46.804
Brief, look, say what's wrong, brief again. Once you're happy, check the facts the C4 way.

26
00:03:47.154 --> 00:03:53.354
No brief, however clear, guarantees no mistakes. Whatever you send out is your responsibility.

27
00:03:54.134 --> 00:04:06.920
Three things to remember: decide whether to delegate; explain the four things, what, for whom, what good looks like, what not to do; revise, then check.

28
00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:16.855
Quick exercise: take a task of your own, write a one-line brief and a four-part brief, ask an AI both, and compare.

29
00:04:17.205 --> 00:04:23.006
How do you know if what comes back is right? Watch C4: checking AI's answers.
